Templates form Moth's string-building engine.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    name = "Priya"

    greeting = [:
        Hello, [name]
    ]
]

Square brackets create the template.

The colon separates its head from its body.

A nested template inserts a value from the surrounding scope.

Templates can build plain text, Markdown, HTML, CSS or another string-based
format. They may be finished during compilation or built at runtime.

HTML pages come from `@entry.moth`. A bare direct [$html:<code>&#91;...&#93;</code>] must fold into
static page output. A direct [$html:<code>~&#91;...&#93;</code>] is an explicit runtime body fragment. Builder metadata uses direct bare [$html:<code>&#91;$in name: ...&#93;</code>] inserts. See @../project-structure/#entry-config (Entry metadata and builder slots) for page metadata. Assigned or returned templates remain ordinary `String` values.
